{"results":[{"url":"https://reverse-video-search.app/who-posted-this-video-first/","final_url":"https://reverse-video-search.app/who-posted-this-video-first/","title":"Who Posted This Video First? Find the Original Uploader","description":"Find who posted a video first: search its frames to find every copy, compare upload dates and open the earliest one. Works with TikTok and YouTube links. Free.","language":"en","text":"Original uploader · Free\n\n# Who Posted This Video First?\n\nTo find who posted a video first, find its copies across the web and compare upload dates. Upload the video or paste a TikTok or YouTube link: we search its frames and mark the earliest copy we find.\n\n* Videos stay on your device\n* Google Lens, Yandex, TinEye\n* Free Lens search · packs from $2.99, no subscription\n\n**Upload a video or screenshot**Drag & drop or tap to choose · MP4, MOV, WebM, JPG, PNG · up to 3 screenshots · videos stay on your device\n\nor\n\nPaste a link\n\nWorks with YouTubeShortsTikTok\n\nNo video handy?\n\n## Key frames\n\nWe search all 3 frames at once.\n\n+ Add another screenshot\n\n* All 3 frames searched at once\n* Matches ranked by platform: TikTok, YouTube, Instagram and more\n* Earliest upload marked\n\nTap the box below to confirm you are human, then your search links will be ready.\n\nHow it works\n\n## How to find the first upload\n\n1. **Add the video**\n\n   Upload the video, or paste a TikTok or YouTube link. A screenshot works too.\n2. **Search its frames**\n\n   Tap Find where this video came from to search several frames at once, or open single frames in Google Lens.\n3. **Check the earliest copy**\n\n   Results show the upload date where the platform provides one, and the earliest copy found is marked. Open it and check the account that posted it.\n\nMatches are grouped by video; the earliest copy found is marked. Sample: *Sintel* © Blender Foundation, CC BY 3.0.\n\n## How to tell the original from a repost\n\n* **Upload date:** the oldest public copy is the strongest lead.\n* **Watermarks and usernames:** a TikTok or Instagram handle in the corner usually points to the creator.\n* **Quality:** reposts are often cropped, re-encoded or zoomed; the original is usually the sharpest and longest version.\n* **The account:** creators have other videos of the same people, place or style. Repost accounts post everything.\n\n## Limits\n\nPrivate, deleted and very new videos cannot be found by search engines, and some sites do not show upload dates. If the earliest copy is itself a repost, search its frames again or check its caption for credit.\n\nFAQ\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### How do I find who posted a video first?\n\nReverse search the video to find its copies, then compare upload dates. Upload the video or paste its link, search its frames, and open the copy marked \"Earliest found\". Then check that account: original creators usually have other videos in the same style, and reposts often credit them in the caption.\n\n### Is the earliest upload always the original?\n\nNot always. Search engines only see public, indexed videos, and some platforms hide upload dates. Treat the earliest copy as the best lead, then confirm with the account itself, watermarks and captions.\n\n### How do I find the original creator of a TikTok repost?\n\nPaste the TikTok link here and search. Look for the earliest copy, a visible @username watermark, or a caption crediting someone. The TikTok reverse video search page has more tips.\n\n### Can I find out who made a viral video?\n\nOften. Viral videos are copied across TikTok, YouTube, Instagram and X. Searching several frames finds copies on all of them, and the oldest ones usually lead to the person who filmed it.\n\n### Is it free?\n\nYes. Frames and the Google Lens, Yandex and TinEye buttons are free with no sign-up. In-site search, which compares results and marks the earliest copy, needs a free Google sign-in.","author":null,"published_date":null,"latency_ms":1296.909285003494,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}